WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:09.360
In early March, West Virginia's teachers
ended a nine-day strike after the

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.920
legislature adopted a 5% boost in
teacher pay. Missing from the headlines

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.830
was much attention to how West Virginia
got here:

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:21.600
West Virginia added staff while losing
students. Over more than two decades, West

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.570
Virginia student enrollment fell by 12%,
but non-teaching staff grew by 10%! In

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:34.230
fact, between 1992 and 2014, real per-pupil spending rose 39%, but real teacher

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:39.719
pay actually fell three percent! Consider
this: A West Virginia teacher would today

00:00:39.719 --> 00:00:44.280
earn more than $63,000 a
year if teacher pay had simply kept pace

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.600
with per-pupil spending. Salaries are
only part of the story, of course. West

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.629
Virginia ranks in the top five
nationally in terms of retirement costs.

00:00:51.629 --> 00:00:56.190
That comes at the expense of take-home
pay. Look, take home pay for West

00:00:56.190 --> 00:01:01.109
Virginia's teachers is pretty dismal, and
this 5% bump certainly helps. But this

00:01:01.109 --> 00:01:06.120
settlement is a costly patch that didn't
address a frustrating status quo.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:12.240
What do you think should be done about teacher pay? Let us know in your comments. Also,

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.460
let us know what other topics you'd like
our scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:24.110
be sure to like and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

